<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!-- A walking person that is a real, MOVING obstacle.

     The geometry is OpenRobotics' Fuel "Walking person" (mesh and inertial
     copied, mesh URIs left pointing at Fuel so they resolve out of the vendored
     fuel_cache like every other downloaded model). What this adds is the
     VelocityControl system, so the model can be driven along a corridor from
     /model/<name>/cmd_vel instead of standing still.

     WHY THIS IS A MODEL AND NOT AN SDF <actor>: actors in gz-sim carry no
     collision geometry at all. They animate, and the LIDAR's rendering-based
     scan may even see them, but contact physics does not, and Nav2's costmaps
     get nothing to avoid. An actor pedestrian is scenery. This is an obstacle.

     WHY IT IS SPAWNED AT MISSION TIME AND NOT PLACED IN hospital_lab.sdf:
     because it moves. Anything in the world file when the map is built gets
     baked into the static map, and a moving obstacle baked into a static map is
     worse than useless - the robot forever avoids the empty space where the
     person WAS and knows nothing about where they now are. Dynamic obstacles
     belong to the live obstacle layer, which is fed by the LIDAR, so the map
     must be built without them. This is exactly the mistake the first hospital
     map made: the pedestrians were static scenery, got mapped as walls, and the
     robot then wedged itself against one of them in the corridor.

     VelocityControl sets the model's velocity directly rather than applying a
     force, which is what keeps an upright 80 kg mesh from toppling or spinning
     the moment it is pushed. -->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <model name="pedestrian">
    <link name="link">
      <inertial>
        <pose>0 0 0.95 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>80.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>27.82</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>24.88</iyy><iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>4.57</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <!-- Collision is an upright cylinder, NOT the person mesh.
           Two reasons, and the first is not cosmetic:

           VelocityControl holds the commanded linear velocity every step,
           including z = 0. The Fuel model's full-mesh collision starts very
           slightly interpenetrating the floor, and with the downward velocity
           pinned at zero the contact solver pushes the model up out of that
           overlap and it never falls back - measured, the pedestrian popped
           from z = 0.010 to z = 0.171 the instant it was first commanded and
           stayed there, walking along on thin air.

           A single primitive whose base sits exactly on z = 0 has no initial
           overlap to be pushed out of, so the pedestrian stays on the floor.
           It is also far cheaper for the solver than a full character mesh.

           Nothing is lost for perception: the LIDAR is a rendering sensor and
           still sees the VISUAL mesh below, so the scan silhouette is the
           person, not the cylinder. Collision geometry only has to be roughly
           person-shaped for contact, and 0.25 m radius by 1.7 m tall is. -->
      <!-- Gravity off, and the collision cylinder deliberately STARTS 0.05 m
           above the floor so it never touches it.

           This is the fix for a pedestrian that walked along climbing into the
           air. VelocityControl pins the commanded velocity every step, z
           included, so a body resting on the ground can be nudged up out of
           contact by the solver but has no downward velocity left to come back
           - the error ratchets. Measured with the mesh collision: z went 0.010
           to 0.171 on the first command. Swapping in a cylinder whose base sat
           exactly on z = 0 did not help either: rest was a clean z = 0.000, but
           five seconds of walking still lifted it to 0.247.

           With no ground contact there is nothing to ratchet against, and with
           gravity off nothing needs the contact in the first place: height is
           whatever it was spawned at, and the pinned velocity holds it there.
           The VISUAL mesh still has its feet at z = 0, so it looks right and
           the LIDAR - a rendering sensor - still sees a person-shaped
           silhouette. The cylinder is only there so the robot cannot drive
           through the body. -->
      <gravity>false</gravity>
      <collision name="body">
        <pose>0 0 0.90 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.25</radius><length>1.70</length></cylinder></geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <pose>0 0 -0.02 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>https://fuel.gazebosim.org/1.0/openrobotics/models/walking%20person/3/files/meshes/walking.dae</uri>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <plugin filename="gz-sim-velocity-control-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::VelocityControl">
    </plugin>

    <!-- Publishes the model pose on /model/<name>/pose. The patrol node needs
         it to hold the walking lane closed-loop: VelocityControl accepts a
         twist but reports nothing back, so without this a pedestrian shoved
         off line during a collision would keep walking down the middle of the
         corridor for the rest of the run, and any later claim that the robot
         "passed a pedestrian in its lane" would be untestable. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-pose-publisher-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::PosePublisher">
      <publish_link_pose>false</publish_link_pose>
      <publish_collision_pose>false</publish_collision_pose>
      <publish_visual_pose>false</publish_visual_pose>
      <publish_model_pose>true</publish_model_pose>
      <use_pose_vector_msg>false</use_pose_vector_msg>
      <update_frequency>20</update_frequency>
    </plugin>
  </model>
</sdf>
